1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,583
Many visitors already have an account with another service.

2
00:00:04,583 --> 00:00:09,213
External authentication lets them use it to sign in to your Orchard Core site.

3
00:00:09,213 --> 00:00:14,560
This video covers the feature, the login page it changes, and the settings that control it.

4
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:23,986
A provider feature, such as Google, Microsoft, GitHub, Meta or X, or an

5
00:00:23,986 --> 00:00:29,314
OpenID Connect provider, adds a button to the login page when it is configured.

6
00:00:29,314 --> 00:00:34,015
The provider confirms who the visitor is, and your site keeps a local

7
00:00:34,015 --> 00:00:35,770
user account for them.

8
00:00:36,520 --> 00:00:40,795
The External Authentication feature holds what every provider shares:

9
00:00:40,795 --> 00:00:44,615
the pages a visitor sees on a first sign-in, a page to manage

10
00:00:44,615 --> 00:00:48,434
linked accounts, and the settings for login and registration.

11
00:00:48,434 --> 00:00:52,310
Each provider adds its own settings, covered in its own video.

12
00:00:53,060 --> 00:00:56,675
Each time a visitor signs in with a provider, the site asks up

13
00:00:56,675 --> 00:00:58,482
to three questions in order:

14
00:00:58,482 --> 00:01:02,469
is this provider account already linked to a local user, does a local

15
00:01:02,469 --> 00:01:07,200
user have the email address the provider shares, and are new registrations allowed?

16
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:13,087
Open Tools, Features.

17
00:01:13,087 --> 00:01:17,849
A search for External Authentication does not list it at first, because the

18
00:01:17,849 --> 00:01:20,730
Visibility filter hides on-demand features:

19
00:01:20,730 --> 00:01:25,080
the ones Orchard Core turns on only when another feature needs them.

20
00:01:25,538 --> 00:01:27,054
Clear Hide on demand.

21
00:01:27,054 --> 00:01:32,049
External Authentication is listed, but its Enable button is inactive, so you cannot

22
00:01:32,049 --> 00:01:33,508
turn it on yourself.

23
00:01:33,982 --> 00:01:36,533
Enable a provider feature instead.

24
00:01:36,533 --> 00:01:43,614
Here that is OpenID Connect Client Integration, with a provider named Example Accounts set up beforehand.

25
00:01:43,614 --> 00:01:49,928
The Google, Microsoft, GitHub, Meta and X provider features depend on External Authentication

26
00:01:49,928 --> 00:01:51,332
in the same way.

27
00:01:51,784 --> 00:01:52,928
Search again.

28
00:01:52,928 --> 00:01:57,567
External Authentication is enabled, and its Disable button is inactive.

29
00:01:57,567 --> 00:02:01,964
A tooltip explains that the feature is enabled based on dependency.

30
00:02:06,166 --> 00:02:09,223
Here is the login page for a signed-out visitor.

31
00:02:09,223 --> 00:02:12,845
The local form is on the left, and on the right, under Use

32
00:02:12,845 --> 00:02:16,863
another service to log in, each configured provider has a button.

33
00:02:16,863 --> 00:02:19,466
This provider is named Example Accounts.

34
00:02:19,954 --> 00:02:22,024
Let's select Example Accounts.

35
00:02:22,024 --> 00:02:27,084
The provider confirms who the visitor is, then sends the browser back to the site.

36
00:02:27,542 --> 00:02:32,727
The site refuses the sign-in with the message New registrations are disabled for this site.

37
00:02:32,727 --> 00:02:37,109
The visitor has no local account, and the registration settings do not allow

38
00:02:37,109 --> 00:02:38,392
one to be created.

39
00:02:42,611 --> 00:02:46,115
To change that, open Settings, Security, Registration.

40
00:02:46,115 --> 00:02:51,663
These External Authentication settings decide what happens when a visitor without a local

41
00:02:51,663 --> 00:02:53,941
account signs in with a provider.

42
00:02:54,395 --> 00:02:57,167
Disable new external registration is selected.

43
00:02:57,167 --> 00:03:01,004
While it is, only people who already have a local account can sign

44
00:03:01,004 --> 00:03:02,337
in with a provider.

45
00:03:02,337 --> 00:03:04,575
That is why the visitor was refused.

46
00:03:05,145 --> 00:03:06,964
Clear it, and select Save.

47
00:03:06,964 --> 00:03:11,285
The site confirms with the message Site settings updated successfully.

48
00:03:11,768 --> 00:03:15,556
Three options control what a first-time visitor is asked for:

49
00:03:15,556 --> 00:03:20,419
Do not ask username, Do not ask email address, and Do not create local password.

50
00:03:20,419 --> 00:03:23,868
Each one removes its fields from the registration form.

51
00:03:24,523 --> 00:03:28,754
When all three are selected and the provider shares an email address, the

52
00:03:28,754 --> 00:03:31,913
site creates the account directly and skips the form.

53
00:03:32,369 --> 00:03:37,384
The last option, Use a script to generate userName based on external provider

54
00:03:37,384 --> 00:03:41,009
claims, lets a developer write the rule for usernames.

55
00:03:45,287 --> 00:03:49,501
Back on the login page, the visitor selects Example Accounts again.

56
00:03:49,501 --> 00:03:54,697
This time the site accepts the sign-in and shows a page headed Create a new account.

57
00:03:55,155 --> 00:04:00,384
The page says the visitor authenticated with the provider, and asks for the rest:

58
00:04:00,384 --> 00:04:06,095
a Username, filled in with a generated value, an Email, a Password, and Confirm password.

59
00:04:06,553 --> 00:04:10,100
The visitor completes the fields and selects Register.

60
00:04:10,100 --> 00:04:15,597
The site creates the local account, links it to the provider, and signs the visitor in.

61
00:04:15,597 --> 00:04:21,863
The visitor's page of external logins then lists the link under Registered Logins, as OpenIdConnect.

62
00:04:26,137 --> 00:04:30,970
Open Settings, Security, User Login, and select the External Login tab.

63
00:04:30,970 --> 00:04:35,992
Use external provider for login applies when only one provider is defined:

64
00:04:35,992 --> 00:04:39,507
the login page then sends visitors straight to it.

65
00:04:39,971 --> 00:04:44,370
The second option, Use a script to set user roles, user claims and

66
00:04:44,370 --> 00:04:50,968
user properties based on external provider claims, runs a developer's script at each external sign-in.

67
00:04:50,968 --> 00:04:53,411
This video does not cover scripts.

68
00:04:53,883 --> 00:04:57,703
Select Use external provider for login, and select Save.

69
00:04:59,275 --> 00:05:03,944
A signed-out visitor who opens the login page does not see the login form.

70
00:05:03,944 --> 00:05:08,439
The browser goes to the provider and comes back with the visitor signed

71
00:05:08,439 --> 00:05:11,825
in, which the list under Registered Logins confirms.

72
00:05:13,825 --> 00:05:18,326
External Authentication is the shared layer behind every sign-in provider:

73
00:05:18,326 --> 00:05:23,502
a button on the login page, rules for first-time visitors, and two groups of settings.

74
00:05:23,502 --> 00:05:24,965
Thanks for watching.

